  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Texas has received $500M in federal contract obligations so far in FY2027. The state recorded 1,389 awards with an average award size of $360K. Obligations are down 99.5% year over year. Federal buying in FY2027 to date is concentrated: the Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $387M, Wired Telecommunications Carriers received $242M, and AT&T ENTERPRISES, LLC received $164M. Historical context shows larger annual totals in prior fiscal years: FY22 was $67.6B, FY23 was $81.9B, FY24 was $59.3B, FY25 was $86.4B, and FY26 was $102B. The rest of buying in FY2027 to date is spread across agencies such as the National Aeronautics and Space Administration with $57.6M and the Department of Justice with $25.8M, and across industries such as Industrial Building Construction with $22.9M and All Other Outpatient Care Centers with $20M. This snapshot reflects partial, fiscal year to date obligations only.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What drove FY2027 to date spending in Texas?",
        "body": "The Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $387M. Wired Telecommunications Carriers received $242M and AT&T ENTERPRISES, LLC received $164M. The National Aeronautics and Space Administration obligated $57.6M and the Department of Justice obligated $25.8M. Industry spending also includes Industrial Building Construction at $22.9M and All Other Outpatient Care Centers at $20M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated is federal buying in Texas?",
        "body": "Buying is highly concentrated in FY2027 to date. The top agency, the Department of Veterans Affairs, accounts for $387M of the $500M total. The top vendor, AT&T ENTERPRISES, LLC, accounts for $164M while the top industry, Wired Telecommunications Carriers, accounts for $242M. The remaining obligations are split among smaller agencies and vendors listed in the top-10 tables."
      }
    ]
